**Competitor 1: Movement To Work - Work Experience**
https://movementtowork.com/

**Strengths

* Clear hero section with a prominent “Join the Movement” CTA
* Strong headline communicates the overall purpose of creating opportunities for young people
* Clear introductory content explains the “no experience, no job” problem and identifies the 16–30 target audience
* Uses varied imagery, cards and separated sections to break up information
* Strong use of impact statistics such as opportunities delivered and positive outcomes, helping establish credibility
* Clearly separates different audiences including young people, employers, partners and members
* Provides direct routes for young people to find opportunities rather than only providing informational content
* Opportunity search allows users to filter by postcode, distance, sector and employer, which makes finding relevant opportunities more efficient
* Opportunities show useful information such as role, employer, location, sector and sometimes salary
* Provides additional learning resources, including AI-related employment support
* Employer logos, government organisations and partner organisations provide strong trust signals
* Real participant stories help demonstrate the potential impact of the programme
* Contact information and further-support routes are available
* Provides links to reports, campaigns, resources and further information for users wanting greater detail
* Strong, engaging workplace imagery helps communicate the employment-focused purpose of the organisation

**Weaknesses

* The homepage serves several different audiences simultaneously, meaning the experience is not primarily focused on a young person trying to become work-ready
* There is a large amount of homepage content, resulting in a long journey before users reach the bottom of the page
* On smaller screens, the amount of content and imagery could result in excessive scrolling and make key information harder to prioritise
* The navigation appears minimal initially, but opening it exposes a large number of links, categories and subcategories that may overwhelm less confident users
* Several navigation options are marked “Soon”, such as case studies and labour-market data, adding options that currently provide no immediate user value
* There is no clear step-by-step journey for a young person, such as discover → prepare → gain experience → develop skills → apply for work
* There is no visual timeline showing what participating in a work-experience programme could actually involve
* Benefits are communicated broadly, but it is not immediately clear which specific skills a participant could gain and how they would develop them
* The national website cannot clearly communicate what happens during the specific Skelmersdale two-week programme
* Important young-person information competes with employer information, campaigns, news, events and organisational information
* Content hierarchy becomes less focused further down the homepage, reducing scannability
* Some sections are conceptually similar, making the page feel repetitive—for example networking/events appear across several areas of the homepage
* The “Free tools & insights” section currently appears to contain copy describing events, while the following “Free events & networking” section contains very similar wording, which can create content confusion
* The opportunity search is useful, but displaying thousands of results across more than 200 pages makes filtering particularly important and could still feel overwhelming to an inexperienced job seeker
* There is limited guidance before the opportunity results to help someone who does not yet know what sector or job they should search for
* There is no obvious interactive method to help users identify their transferable skills before exploring roles
* Travel is handled mainly through postcode/distance filtering rather than helping users understand realistic transport options or travel barriers
* There is no obvious personalised explanation of “Is this programme right for me?”
* The homepage CTA “Join the Movement” could be ambiguous for young people because the site also uses “join” in relation to employers and organisational membership

**Competitor 2: BAE SYSTEMS - Tackling youth unemployment**
https://careers.baesystems.com/locations/uk/movement-to-work

**Strengths
* Strong association with a recognised employer helps establish credibility and trust
* Clearly identifies the page as being related to BAE Systems and Movement to Work
* Uses prominent impact statistics to demonstrate real programme outcomes
* Shows approximately 1,000 completed placements, 600+ positive job or education outcomes and 320 participants hired by BAE Systems
* Provides tangible evidence that Movement to Work can lead to employment and apprenticeship opportunities
* Includes video content to make the programme more engaging than text alone
* Uses imagery and visual sections to break up large amounts of written content
* Real employee stories provide social proof and demonstrate possible career pathways
* Provides links to the official Movement to Work website and further work-experience information
* Includes a direct contact route through the Movement to Work team
* Clearly communicates the wider value of the programme to employers
* Strong use of real outcomes helps make the programme feel legitimate rather than purely promotional

**Weaknesses
* The intended audience is unclear because the page appears to discuss opportunities for young people but then focuses heavily on employers
* A major section directly asks employers whether they would like to get involved, shifting attention away from young people looking for work experience
* There is no clear primary CTA for a young person wanting to take part in Movement to Work
* The page does not clearly explain how a young person can join or what their first step should be
* There is no clear programme timeline showing what a Movement to Work placement involves
* The specific skills participants could gain are not clearly presented
* There is little practical information explaining what a participant will actually do
* Important information is spread across a long page, requiring excessive scrolling
* The content hierarchy becomes less focused further down the page
* Large amounts of employer-focused written content may be difficult to scan
* The Movement to Work section eventually transitions into general BAE Systems employee stories, many of which are not directly connected to Movement to Work
* The large number of employee stories can distract from the original purpose of the page
* Some stories relate to graduates, experienced employees and international careers rather than the target Movement to Work audience
* The page does not provide a clear journey such as Discover → Join → Complete Placement → Develop Skills → Find Work
* It does not clearly explain eligibility for young participants
* There is no clear explanation of how long a placement lasts
* There is no clear breakdown of the support participants receive with CVs, interviews or job applications
* Transferable skills are not explained from the participant's perspective
* There is no dedicated local travel information connected to opportunities
* The interface provides limited interaction for someone trying to determine whether Movement to Work is suitable for them
* The page feels like an extension of the wider BAE Careers website rather than a focused Movement to Work experience
* The large site-wide footer and navigation introduce many unrelated links, which can distract users from the primary task

Competitor 3: Get My FirstJob - About Movement to Work
https://www.getmyfirstjob.co.uk/employers/details/61

* The page immediately identifies Movement to Work and explains its purpose
* The introductory content clearly explains the problem of young people facing the "no experience, no job" cycle
* Clearly identifies the target age range of 16–30
* Explains that Movement to Work works with major UK employers to provide work-experience opportunities
* A clear "View Current Opportunities" action gives users a direct route towards available roles
* Uses statistics and recognisable employer names to establish trust and legitimacy
* Explains that opportunities are intended to develop transferable skills and strengthen participants' CVs
* Provides a dedicated section explaining what Movement to Work does
* Includes quotes from Movement to Work representatives, adding credibility and human context
* Clearly links the programme to employability, work experience and future careers
* Includes a Career Advisor chatbot, giving users another way to seek support
* Provides links to search opportunities, browse industries and take a career quiz
* The wider platform offers several routes for users who may not yet know which career to pursue
* Candidate and employer areas are clearly labelled within the wider navigation
* Social media, contact, privacy and accreditation information provide additional trust signals
* The page is responsive and presents content in a format that can be accessed across different devices
* The wider platform also promotes an AI-for-jobseekers programme covering CVs, interviews and employer research, which aligns strongly with employability support

**Weaknesses
* The hero area identifies Movement to Work but gives limited information about what the user should do immediately after arriving
* The page does not clearly explain what a participant will actually experience during a placement
* There is no programme timeline or step-by-step explanation of the participant journey
* Specific skills are discussed broadly as "transferable skills" but are not broken down into practical examples
* There is limited explanation of how participants develop those skills during the programme
* The page does not clearly explain what happens after completing a placement
* There is no obvious "Is this right for me?" section for users who are unsure whether they should take part
* The page currently states that there are no matching vacancies, which can create a dead end for users who arrived specifically looking for Movement to Work opportunities
* Although a link to search all opportunities is provided, the user is moved away from the Movement to Work context rather than being given alternative relevant options directly
* No ambassadors are currently associated with Movement to Work, removing a potential source of participant stories or social proof
* Large areas of written content may be difficult to scan for users with lower digital confidence
* Important information such as eligibility, benefits and next steps is presented mainly through text rather than visual summaries
* The page has little visual explanation of the participant journey
* There is no clear breakdown of CV support, interview preparation or application guidance on the Movement to Work page itself
* Travel information is not connected directly to Movement to Work opportunities
* There is no localised information for users such as nearby employers, sectors or transport options
* Candidate and employer content exist within the same wider platform, which can make the overall information architecture feel broad
* The site contains many navigation routes such as search, ambassadors, industries, employers, news, account features and career tools, which may be overwhelming for users with a simple goal
* The chatbot is persistent throughout the page and may compete visually with the main content
* The chatbot's value is not immediately clear before interaction
* The footer contains a large number of links and categories, creating another dense information area at the end of the page

**Additional Visual Observations

* Some imagery appears disconnected from the surrounding content rather than being used to communicate information
* Large areas of whitespace can increase scrolling without necessarily improving understanding
* Some buttons have limited visual prominence compared with surrounding content
* Certain icons may not clearly communicate the action they represent
* The hamburger menu may be less convenient for one-handed mobile use because of its positioning
* Typography and content density can feel inconsistent between different areas of the platform
* Some opportunity cards contain several competing pieces of information, reducing visual hierarchy
* The footer does not strongly separate itself visually from the main page content

